A||N||22||RussB||Bleeding the clutch please help||||5||
Z||000000||RussB||10-05-2001||01:17 PM||||I have replaced my Master, Slave and hydraulic line. But can not get it bled. I am sure this is the problem I can only get the slave to throw about a ½ in. I bleed my line using Archie’s way, after four days of trying it the text book way, but still will not work. All of my parts were bought from the Fiero store. My car has been sitting since July. Please Help!<P>------------------<BR>Black 88 2.5L 5SPD||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indyrestoguy_JR||10-05-2001||01:31 PM||||Try jacking the car up in the rear till the slave is above the level of master and leave system closed. pump pedal a few times and leave it alone for a couple of hours. Do not pump pedal yet! Open bleeder and bleed with an assistant as you would brakes. Note: do this slowly with the pedal, avoid fast movement!All the air should run to the highest point in the lines. This should eliminate the majority of air in system.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierose||10-05-2001||07:31 PM||||Sure you don't have a bent pedal, or your banjo being installed upside down.  That's all on the archie site.  there's also a replacement bushing I got from rodney dickmans site that takes some of the play out of the clutch.  Just some ideas.  I still have problems with my clutch.  It's the one thing i hate about fiero's.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||10-05-2001||10:13 PM||||I've had the best luck using vacuum. You can get a bleeder "thingie" for $5 or $6. It's a plastic cup with a lid and two hoses to seperate the liquid from the vacuum source. You can get a hand vacuum pump fairly reasonable. I went the cheap route and used engine vacuum.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Just make sure your full of fluid. Hook it up, apply vacuum and open up the bleeder. You should see some air bubbles come out with the fluid. Close the bleeder, check the fluid and repeat a couple of times.<P>Also, the bleeder seems to leak air around the threads. If you're getting a lot of air it may be coming from there and not getting a good vacuum on the system. I put some teflon tape on the bleeder to help this...||||reg||1
Z||000004||samoht||10-05-2001||10:22 PM||||i am in the same postion, any help please..  I replaced the master and slave and steel bracket, new bushing on pedal to.. but cant get but and inch or two of pedal... <P><BR>85 GT 3.2||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||10-05-2001||10:23 PM||||The MityVac has always worked well for me.  I rebuilt my clutch mater cylinder and put on a new slave, no problems.  Just don't let the reservoir run dry or you have to start all over again.  An assistant helps.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||10-07-2001||08:20 AM||||Well, is it working now?||||reg||1
Z||000007||2xsess||10-07-2001||11:33 AM||||I tried many different ways of bleeding my clutch when I replaced all that stuff too. <P>Here's what I found that worked the best and the easiest.<BR>open the cap on the master, open the bleeder on the slave (making sure drip pan is under car), keep an eye on the master, DO NOT let it empty, as you pace back an forth keeping an eye on the master and slave, you will notice air bubbles coming out of the lsave, do this for about 15-20 minutes and then see how it works. That's what I did and haven't had any problems.<P>Good Luck<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">           <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>Visit My Website</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||samoht||10-07-2001||05:33 PM||||that s what I just did and the gravity bleed worked great.. I think now I have moistur ein the line cause I had it open for 3 days...   a lirttle play left so I am going to replace all the fliud, i will let you know how it comes out||||reg||1
Z||000009||RussB||10-09-2001||01:46 AM||||I have tried leaving the system open for 1 hour with no luck. I went out and bought a brake bleeder I will let you know how it works thanks for all of the suggestions so far. How can you tell if your clutch pedal is bent without buying a new one and comparing them. is there a good way to check for leaks. I figure soapy water would not be a good idea.<P>------------------<BR>Black 88 2.5L 5SPD||||reg||8
Z||000010||TooManyFieros||10-10-2001||02:37 PM||||bump||||reg||1
Z||000011||Indiana_resto_guy||10-10-2001||03:08 PM||||Being an 88, I would say that your pedal is steel. Use a magnet to check it with. The aluminum ones have a tendancy to bend.<BR>What brand of parts did you buy to replace the bad parts with?||||reg||1
Z||000012||CW85T-Type||10-10-2001||04:25 PM||||my way of bleeding may not be very good, but it worked for me.... and is still working...<P>First off, if you have a alum pedal, replace it.  I had a alum pedal and after replacing it the clutch disingages perfectly.<P>To bleed I first took the slave off the bracket and took the pushrod out. I had a helper push the clutch (so the piston was all the way out (has a snap ring to keep the piston from coming out) I then put the cylinder at a angle so the bleeder valve is above the piston (point the piston side down) and opened up the bleeder while a helper pushed the clutch.  I made sure to keep the resovier full.  After a few strokes When I closed the bleeder the pedal would not move at all (the whole system was locked up since the piston was all the way out.  there was no longer any air in the system.  I put it back together, pulling some fluid out of the resovire as when the piston goes back in it will push fluid out.  <P>It works perfectly now....<P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||RussB||10-19-2001||03:07 AM||||I have tried all of your suggestions, and nothing has worked. So I took a week off to stop being so frustrated. After careful consideration I have decided to try and replace my slave first with a GM one. If that doesn't do it then I will get a GM master. A special thanks to Jim for his emails, although they have not helped yet I think that when I get the GM parts in they will.||||reg||1
Z||000014||WampusCat||10-19-2001||06:09 PM||||Can someone describe or show a picture of a bent clutch pedal?  I have the steel pedal but it looks bent to me.  I've bled the system and I can barely shift between gears with the engine idling and the pedal all the way to the floor.  I can't shift at all with the pedal 1/2" off the floor.   Is the arm between the footpad and the pivot supposed to be parallel to the brake pedal arm?  Mine is angled/twisted toward the driver's side of the car<p>[This message has been edited by WampusCat (edited 10-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||FieroBUZZ||10-19-2001||06:44 PM||||As far as I know your clutch pedal should sit about 1 inch higher than the brake pedal. There is a bracket which seems to be the bending culprit, so it may be bent even if you have a steel pedal.<BR>Gary||||reg||1
Z||000016||RussB||10-20-2001||02:50 PM||||Where is this bracket which seems to be the bending culprit, is it the one that the banjo hooks up to. In other words this bracket can be bent and your pedal will still be an 1 higher than the brake pedal. <P>------------------<BR>Black 88 2.5L 5SPD||||reg||1
Z||000017||mrfiero||10-20-2001||03:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Indiana_resto_guy:<BR><B>What brand of parts did you buy to replace the bad parts with?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Since he said they are from the Fiero Store I will assume that they are the aftermarket crap that I rant about every so often.  I believe the Fiero Store sells Wagner stuff, but I'm not sure (all of the aftermarket slaves are the same).<P>That said.....I will 'go out on a limb' here and say you need new hydraulics!  Buy only genuine GM slave and master cylinders and your life will become easier.....especially when it comes to bleeding the system.  I just installed a brand new clutch and brand new flywheel along with a brand new GM slave and brand new GM master cylinder.....it took all of 20 minutes to bleed the thing.  It shifts nice and smooth now with no fanfare. <P>I went through this same scenario many years ago.......a different Fiero, but a new clutch and new NAPA slave & master.  I never did get it to bleed properly and it led to the premature failure of the new clutch.  <P>Do yourself a big favor and replace those slave and master cylinders with GM parts......maybe you can return them to the Fiero Store for a refund? <P>Good luck.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||mrfiero||10-20-2001||03:45 PM||||Whoops!  Double post.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 10-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||Joe Torma||10-20-2001||04:27 PM||||I just replaced the clutch master cylender on my car...GM installed the rod upside down and the thing was leaking on the padding.  Messy.  (I didn't get a GM one, hopefully I will be ok for a while!)<P>So, I tried sucking air out of the system for about 15 minutes with the mightyvac.  I used teflon tape on the bleeder threads.  No luck. But was sure there was no air from the master cyl, back to the bleeder.<P>But I did have idea what was wrong. Air in the slave.  I had lots of fluid with no bubbles in the clear tube attaced to the bleeder. I cracked the bleeder open, pushed the rod/piston of the slave IN ALL THE WAY until all the air bubbles traveled up the tube and slowly let the piston out and tightened the bleeder.  <P>I have never had a better feeling in the clutch!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 10-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||RussB||11-02-2001||12:52 PM||||Good news all. I have replaced my master and slave cylinders with GM parts and problem solved. In fact it only took me an hour to take the old master and slave out and bleed the system. My only regret is that it took me 3 months to figure out to buy GM parts.<P>------------------<BR>Black 88 2.5L 5SPD||||reg||6
Z||000021||SLYGUY||11-02-2001||01:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 2birds:<BR><B>The MityVac has always worked well for me.  I rebuilt my clutch mater cylinder and put on a new slave, no problems.  Just don't let the reservoir run dry or you have to start all over again.  An assistant helps.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I was having a similar problem. <BR>I had to have the clutch pedal buried into the carpet to get the clutch disengaged enough to shift. I figured I had air in the system so I attempted to bleed it. This resulted in a totally lifeless pedal and a full clutch engagement. <BR>The fluid in the reservoir looked miserable. I took the master cylinder down to Autozone and bought a rebuilt....BIG mistake. The rebuilt was every bit as worthless as my worn out original. After 5-7 hours of attempting to restore a clutch through bleeding the system I broke down and bought a new GM unit. <P>Put the new master in and bled it with a Mityvac in about 20 mins. The clutch isn't as sweet as in other stick shift cars I've driven, but it works. I'm used to the car being a little notchy to shift so I'm satisfied for now.<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000022||RussB||11-03-2001||12:41 AM||||The clutch isn't as sweet as in other stick shift cars I've driven, but it works. I'm used to the car being a little notchy to shift so I'm satisfied for now.<P>Sly[/B][/QUOTE]<P>Sly maybe your slave is bad also, because my clutch feels better now then it ever did. Make sure there is absolutely no fluid coming out of the slave. The outside of the slave jacket should be dry. My original slave had a leak that appeared to be a pinhole leak in the slave jacket. I think that caused my master to go bad.<P><P>------------------<BR>Black 88 2.5L 5SPD||||reg||1
